WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.337
Here is a simple Spark notebook workflow.

00:00:02.337 --> 00:00:04.497
A file is read into a DataFrame.

00:00:04.497 --> 00:00:07.412
The DataFrame is registered as a temporary view.

00:00:07.412 --> 00:00:10.047
Then Spark SQL queries that view.

00:00:10.047 --> 00:00:15.522
This code is not meant for the browser practice engine because it requires a real Spark session.

00:00:15.522 --> 00:00:21.802
The important idea is the connection between files, DataFrames, temporary views, and SQL.

00:00:21.802 --> 00:00:26.282
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:26.282 --> 00:00:32.102
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:32.102 --> 00:00:34.407
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:34.407 --> 00:00:40.827
In a real setting such as a healthcare analytics platform processing large longitudinal event tables,

00:00:40.827 --> 00:00:46.582
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:46.582 --> 00:00:48.182
before discussing syntax.

00:00:48.182 --> 00:00:53.910
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
